{
  "id": "Compound::DB00678 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Losartan is classified as an Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonist, and multiple related compounds—Valsartan, Telmisartan, and Irbesartan—share this classification and are known to treat hypertension. The evidence suggests that these drugs, through their common mechanism of action, exert antihypertensive effects by blocking the action of angiotensin II, a hormone that contributes to vasoconstriction and fluid retention, both of which increase blood pressure. The lowest common ancestors of Losartan and its related drugs point to broader categories like Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists and Angiotensin Ii Receptor Antagonists, reinforcing the shared pharmacological pathway. The consistent treatment effect across these compounds supports the hypothesis that Losartan, through its inclusion in this class, may also be effective in managing hypertension. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of receptor-targeted mechanisms in drug repositioning, with the underlying mechanism being the modulation of angiotensin II signaling.",
